spec: none — a refutation, recorded where the cast is

why: the operator pointed at Scala 3's generalized method syntax
  (type clauses after term clauses), whose own example is
  `def getOrElse(k: Key)[V >: k.Value](default: V): V` — the exact
  shape of `Refs.handle`'s `slot`, which holds the one cast this
  repository documents as unavoidable.

what was tried, and what it says (probes in scratchpad/probe):
  - Heap.scala: `Ref` carrying `type Value`, heap `Map[Ref, Slot]`,
    `def slot(h, c: Ref)[V >: c.Value]`. REFUSED: `s.value` has type
    `s.ref.Value` and nothing proves `s.ref` is `c`.
  - Heap2.scala: the heap as a DEPENDENT FUNCTION,
    `(c: Ref) => Option[c.Value]`, which a Map cannot be. REFUSED at
    `put`: inside the lambda the key is `k`, the value is `c.Value`,
    and `k eq c` is a run-time fact the types never learn.

  So the feature fixes SIGNATURES and not STORAGE: it lets a type
  depend on a term, and does not recover what erasure already threw
  away. The cast in Refs is about the second. Refs.scala's comment
  already argues a type cannot list cells that do not exist yet; this
  adds the fact that the 3.x feature which looks like it would remove
  the cast does not, so the next reader does not spend the evening
  finding out.

what lands: the finding, in the doc comment beside the cast and in
  BACKLOG. No code changes — there is nothing to change.

next: comment, backlog, gate
